[Music]  the fun of a runaway train  just affair  remember this part from Red Dead  Redemption the part where John Marston  rides into Mexico to the evocative  lyrics of Jose Gonzales the part where  the world felt like a character in  itself like it was reacting to your  arrival and although other players would  experience this same moment it was  ultimately yours and yours alone  Rockstar has made its entire sequel  about moments like this with Red Dead  Redemption to the studio has created a  responsive world that recognizes the  player adapts and yields appropriate  results Rockstar has made the America of  1899 a character of its own so to speak  it is nuanced and fickle  it contains multitudes and it represents  a significant leap in open-world design  [Music]  it's on  [Music]  at Eurogamer Expo 2017 Bioshock director  Ken Levine took the stage to talk about  his career and his new studio ghost  story games was created from the remains  of irrational games early last year and  it signaled a shift from linear shooters  to more emergent narratives so there's a  term we have of the studio which is  called radical recognition and when that  means is if the gamer does something the  game should recognize it and the game  should talk about it like there was a  thing back in System Shock 1 you make  this jump over this pit and there's a  line they recorded in a part of show  tangos nice jump that was a moment which  I never seen before with the game  recognized some just think you did in  the world right and so one of the most  important things about what we're doing  now is this concept of radical  recognition in short ghost story wants  to show the player that the game world  cares when asked about good examples of  this tenant in recent games Levine  mentioned 2014's Middle Earth shadow of  Mordor famed for its dynamic nemesis  system  shadow of Mordor creates personalities  for its army of Orcs based on their  interactions with the player finish off  an orc captain with an arrow in his head  and he'll return later with a metal  plate attached to his skull he'll also  remember the encounter and taunt you  accordingly  to paraphrase Levine the Nemesis system  is a focused rudimentary approach to  that idea of radical recognition  shadow of Mordor pays attention to the  player's actions and it responds in turn  I can't wait for Levine to play Red Dead  Redemption too much like rockstars  previous titles Red Dead 2 has a  scripted main storyline with concrete  characters and a linear narrative it  follows one Arthur Morgan as he escapes  the long arm of the law in the American  Wild West but after 50 hours in  rockstars Western it's clear the team  wanted to create a world with that same  kind of digital memory Levine described  unlike rockstars previous game Red Dead  2 really seems to care about your  existence more than that it seems to  care about how you choose to exist if  you act like an outlaw the game will  make you feel like one  if you try your best to be a decent  person the world's inhabitants will  treat you accordingly to give a good  example I'll use one of my favorite  personal anecdotes early in my  playthrough an npc recognized Arthur  from his past not wanting him to alert  the authorities I naturally gave chase  the only problem was in all the  confusion I took somebody else's horse I  caught up to the man threatened him and  I felt safer than I had before nothing  felt too out of the ordinary but then I  got back to town and this is what  happened next it was that return and  that man recognizing me and recognizing  my semi good deed that really made the  whole thing shine this kind of story  isn't new The Witcher 3 Excel that  reminding you of choices you made  throughout Geralt adventure but these  little incidental reminders happen  constantly in Red Dead 2 they occur so  often that they become the norm it was  still impressive don't get me wrong but  it just didn't stand out anymore it felt  natural it felt organic but the fun in  Red Dead 2 comes from experiencing those  moments yourself so I'll let you do just  that  we've navigated countless branching  narratives and games we've explored  massive sandbox landscapes we've built  an army of custom characters with  distinct personalities powers and styles  by now video games have developed a  strong grasp on player choice what most  games don't firmly grasp however is  consequence and really choice is nothing  without consequence and that's why Red  Dead 2 is so impressive it regularly  shows us the aftermath of the decisions  we make and how they affect the world  and the people around us these  consequences can be something relatively  small the town drunk getting pissed at  you for staring too long for example or  it can be something more pervasive if  you constantly rob stagecoaches or kill  innocent people the authorities  recognize you as an outlaw you'll not  only be wanted in the map's respective  provinces but townsfolk avoid you store  owners are on alert and all in all  inhabitants are just less trusting Red  Dead 2 not only gives you the choice to  be evil but makes you feel evil as a  result I recently read about a program  at the University of Copenhagen called  Super Mario evolution it records various  play styles in the flash game infinite  mario bros by compiling these results it  then builds new levels to encourage or  oppose these play styles in the abstract  for the research groups paper Julian -  Elias outlined one of the major benefits  of developing such a program while there  is limited demand in the games industry  for higher performing opponents in most  games genre x' there is a demand for  more interesting npcs opponents allies  sidekicks etc for better ways of  adapting the game to the player so  although it's impactful to design a game  that can make the player react it's  another challenge entirely to make a  world that reacts to the player but like  the devs that worked on shadow of Mordor  and the researchers behind Super Mario  evolution Rockstar Games knows the value  of a reactive world Red Dead - builds  expectations so well that even when I'm  doing something menial I feel as if the  world is paying attention I came across  this abandoned wagon recently and as I  took the time to investigate  I felt genuine paranoia maybe someone  who's waiting to ambush me or maybe  there's a corpse nearby or maybe there's  something sinister hiding behind that  claw  flapping in the wind but there was  nothing there it was just an abandoned  wagon I don't know why it was there or  whose it was but regardless I continued  on my way unable to shake the feeling  that someone or something was watching  me that whole time we come into every  game with an element of curiosity but  this is one of those few times I felt as  if a game was curious about me as I  mentioned there are times when Red Dead  2 borders on eerie but there are other  times this radical recognition results  in excitement or hilarity at one point I  climbed a water tower to avoid the  authorities canvassing the crime scene  below they noticed me and this is what  happened what you know about it know by  this point Red Dead 2 had built up its  world so well that I not only foresaw  the consequences of the murders I  committed but also knew my best chance  of avoiding them what followed was a  memorable story involving a tense  standoff and a really dumb deputy we  know that every form of entertainment  requires a willing suspension of  disbelief we acknowledge that we're  staring at a screen or reading words on  a page and we surrender to the story  being told it's part of the psychology  of enjoying media it's an agreement  between the creator and the viewer that  there is artifice here but we'll ignore  it as best we can and let our  imaginations do their things Red Dead  Redemption 2 tries to ask less of you in  this regard than almost any other game  I've played I always know I'm playing a  game of course but I will say it is  magnitudes easier to suspend that  disbelief because I expect NPCs to  recognize me I expect my actions to have  ripple effects I expect the world to  change as I change alongside it no  shortage of hyperbole has been spent on  describing open-world games for the last  decade they've transported us and  explored player agency doing their best  to disguise their boundaries and fill in  the cracks before we notice them we use  terms such as immersive dynamic living  and breathing in many cases games have  come close to actually earning these  badges but I'm not sure I've played a  game that's come as close as Red Dead  Redemption to  it's a masterclass in writing animation  and most of all world design it's a  stunning example of how open worlds can  encourage certain types of behaviors yes  but also how they can react to certain  types of behavior it's a towering  achievement and we'll be talking about  it for a long time  [Music]

